To ensure BGP automatically shuts down a neighbor connection when BFD fails, use the following configuration:

```cli
configure router bgp
    group "your-group-name"
        neighbor 192.0.2.1
            bfd-enable
        exit
    exit
exit
```

**Explanation:**  
The `bfd-enable` command directly binds the BGP neighbor session state to the BFD session status. If BFD fails, BGP will immediately tear down the neighbor connection—no additional peer-tracking or shutdown commands are needed. Replace `your-group-name` and `192.0.2.1` with your actual BGP group name and neighbor IP.

**Critical Pre-requisite:**  
BFD must be fully configured on the interface used by this BGP neighbor (e.g., under `config>router>interface>bfd`). Without active BFD on the interface, `bfd-enable` won't function.
