To handle BGP updates containing your own AS number in the AS_PATH without tearing down the session, use the `local-as` command with the `private` option. This configuration masks your global AS number for the specific neighbor, preventing the router from recognizing its own AS in the AS_PATH and thus avoiding session resets.

```cli
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        neighbor <neighbor-ip>
            local-as <dummy-as> private
```

**Variables**:
- `<group-name>`: Name of the BGP group.
- `<neighbor-ip>`: IP address of the neighbor.
- `<dummy-as>`: A temporary, unused AS number (e.g., `64512`).

**Explanation**:
- The `local-as` command configures a virtual AS for the session, replacing your global AS.
- The `private` option hides this virtual AS in paths advertised to the peer.
- Routes received with your global AS in the AS_PATH will no longer trigger loop detection, keeping the session active.
